CYCLE=0
IDEA=$'teammate-death-diagnostics — capture durable diagnostics when an SDK teammate dies\n\n>> Goal / user-visible outcome\n\nWhen an SDK teammate dies (the node `claude` CLI subprocess exits non-zero and the\nSDK raises ProcessError), the next OCCURRENCE must be self-explaining. Today a death\nproduces an opaque envelope to the lead ("...crashed: Command failed with exit code 1")\nand we have NO record of WHY: the subprocess stderr is discarded, the death site logs\nnothing, and we never capture what the teammate was doing. Operators (and the\ncoordinator) should be able to read, after the fact, exactly why a teammate died.\n\nThis is DIAGNOSTIC TELEMETRY, not a behavioral fix. We are deliberately NOT trying to\nprevent the death or change control flow — we are making the next organic death\ndiagnosable. It closes two backlog items: the 2026-06-04 "SDK teammate dies on a\nnon-zero Bash exit" entry (its stated root cause is a MISDIAGNOSIS — see below) and the\n2026-05-24 "subprocess death undiagnosable because we discard stderr" entry.\n\n>> Three deliverables (priority order)\n\n1. PER-TEAMMATE STDERR RING BUFFER (the load-bearing piece). Register a `stderr`\n   callback on the SDK client options so the node CLI\'s stderr is captured into a\n   bounded ring buffer (last ~50 lines, also byte-capped) on the SdkTeammate. On death,\n   the redacted tail is attached to the death record. This is the ONLY way to see why\n   the CLI exits.\n\n2. LAST-TOOL-BEFORE-DEATH CONTEXT. Snapshot what the teammate was doing when it died —\n   especially the last Bash command + its outcome, plus any in-flight tool — into the\n   death record. This directly tests the backlog\'s assumed "non-zero Bash exit"\n   correlation: next death, we will SEE whether a Bash exit-1 was actually in flight.\n\n3. LOG + ATTACH AT THE DEATH SITE. At the ProcessError/CLIConnectionError/BrokenPipe\n   match site, emit a WARNING with exc class name, exit_code, the stderr tail, and the\n   last-tool context — and attach the same to the broker death record so it surfaces on\n   the dashboard and in the transcript.\n\n>> SDK-flow context — REQUIRED reading for whoever writes the spec\n\nThe implementor will NOT know how claude-agent-sdk drives teammates. The spec must\ncarry this so the implementor needs zero SDK-internals discovery.\n\n- claude-crew\'s SdkTeammate (`claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py`) drives a node `claude` CLI\n  subprocess via the SDK\'s ClaudeSDKClient. Options are built around line 1376\n  (`options = ClaudeAgentOptions(**opts_kwargs)`) then `async with\n  ClaudeSDKClient(options=options) as client:` (~1378).\n\n- The SDK\'s SubprocessCLITransport spawns the node process and pipes stderr ONLY when a\n  callback is registered:\n    subprocess_cli.py:447  →  stderr_dest = PIPE if self._options.stderr is not None else None\n    subprocess_cli.py:493-506  →  _handle_stderr() reads lines and calls self._options.stderr(line_str)\n  claude-crew currently passes NO `stderr` callback, so the CLI\'s stderr is inherited\n  into the server\'s own stream — unattributed and effectively lost. ClaudeAgentOptions\n  has a `stderr` field that takes a callback `Callable[[str], None]` invoked per line.\n  THIS is the supported hook to add.\n\n- When the node CLI exits non-zero, the transport raises\n    ProcessError("Command failed with exit code N", exit_code=N)   (subprocess_cli.py:673-676)\n  surfacing during the receive_response() drain. ProcessError carries `.exit_code` and\n  `.stderr` fields (_errors.py:25-40), BUT the transport raises it with stderr=None — so\n  the exception itself does NOT carry the why. We must capture stderr ourselves via the\n  callback above.\n\n- claude-crew catches it at the generic except arm in `_handle_one_turn`\n  (sdk_teammate.py:1479-1491): matches ProcessError/CLIConnectionError/BrokenPipe by\n  class name, sets _death_suspected=True, stores the in-flight envelope, returns\n  SILENTLY — no log of the exception, exit_code, or context. The liveness poll\n  (sdk_teammate.py:1168-1207) then calls broker._handle_teammate_death(exit_code=...).\n\n- The death record / tombstone is built in `broker.py` `_tombstone_teammate`\n  (~366-560); TeammateInfo death-record fields are ~46-88. New diagnostic fields attach\n  here and flow to the dashboard/transcript.\n\n>> Expected vs actual\n\nACTUAL: a ProcessError death → opaque "crashed: Command failed with exit code 1"\nenvelope, teammate tombstoned, zero record of why (stderr discarded, death site silent,\nno tool context).\n\nEXPECTED: on death we capture + persist (a) last ~50 lines of CLI stderr (redacted),\n(b) last tool invoked (esp. last Bash command + outcome) + any in-flight tool at death,\n(c) a WARNING at the death site with exc class + exit_code + stderr tail + last-tool.\nAll attached to the death record; visible on the dashboard and in the transcript.\n\n>> Constraints / non-negotiables\n\n- SECRETS: stderr can contain tokens/env. It MUST go through `claude_crew/redaction.py`\n  (`redact_error` / `redact_output`, honor REDACTION_VERSION) before ANY persistence\n  (death record, transcript, dashboard).\n- BOUNDED: ring buffer uses a deque(maxlen=...) plus a byte cap — never unbounded.\n- ADDITIVE ONLY: do not change death/tombstone control flow or existing error-envelope\n  semantics. Telemetry only.\n- The stderr callback must be cheap, non-blocking, and never raise out of itself (a\n  raising callback could destabilize the SDK\'s stderr reader task). Just append.\n- Reuse existing tracking: `_tool_uses` (in-flight dict), `_last_tool_completed`, and the\n  PreToolUse/PostToolUse hooks (F8) already in sdk_teammate — do not build a parallel\n  tool tracker.\n\n>> Verification (two layers, happy + sad — per repo standards)\n\n- Implementation layer (stub/unit): ring buffer is bounded + redacted; death-record\n  attachment populated by injecting a ProcessError and feeding synthetic stderr lines.\n  Sad: secret-bearing stderr → assert redacted; empty stderr → graceful empty/None, no\n  crash.\n- Integration layer: a GATED live SDK test (CLAUDE_CREW_LIVE_TESTS=1) that spawns a real\n  teammate, runs an ordinary turn, and asserts the stderr callback received normal CLI\n  stderr lines — proves the wiring end to end WITHOUT needing to force a crash. Plus a\n  stub-mode broker test asserting death-record fields populate on tombstone.\n- Honor CLAUDE.md test conventions: bounded async-iterator drains; HOME-monkeypatch SDK\n  auth preservation for live tests; run the FULL `uv run pytest` at the validation gate\n  (this touches widely-consumed teammate/broker behavior).\n\n>> Explicitly OUT OF SCOPE (do not chase)\n\n- Do NOT fix or prevent the node CLI exiting non-zero — the distal cause is unknown;\n  this telemetry is how we will learn it.\n- Do NOT add session-resume / retry-on-ProcessError resilience — separate future call.\n- Do NOT implement the backlog\'s "catch the non-zero-exit tool error and feed it back as\n  the tool result" — that is a MISDIAGNOSIS. claude-crew never sees individual tool\n  results; by the time we catch ProcessError the subprocess is already dead. There is no\n  live model to feed.\n- No dashboard redesign — surfacing the new fields in the existing death/status\n  rendering is sufficient.\n\n>> Pointers to read\n\n- claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py — options (~1376), generic except arm (1479-1491),\n  liveness poll (1168-1207), hooks/tool tracking, status_snapshot.\n- claude_crew/broker.py — _tombstone_teammate (~366-560), TeammateInfo fields (~46-88).\n- claude_crew/redaction.py — redact_error, redact_output, REDACTION_VERSION.\n- .venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/claude_agent_sdk/_internal/transport/subprocess_cli.py\n  (447-468, 493-510, 668-676) and _errors.py (25-40) — for UNDERSTANDING only; the SDK\n  is a dependency, NOT editable.\n- CLAUDE.md (project) — test conventions, multi-instance dashboard rule, full-suite gate.\n\n>> IMPORTANT — instruction about the implementor\n\nThe implementor will be a LOCAL model (qwen36) with NO prior knowledge of\nclaude-agent-sdk internals or how SDK teammates wire stderr / process lifecycle. Write\nthe spec so the implementor needs ZERO SDK-internals discovery: embed the exact\nfile:line anchors, the exact ClaudeAgentOptions field name + callback signature, the\nprecise registration point, code-level pseudocode for the ring buffer + redaction +\ndeath-record attachment, and the exact test assertions. Treat the spec as a\ntranscription target, not a research prompt. Where the implementor must understand\nbehavior it cannot change (the SDK transport), state the behavior as a given fact with\nthe file:line, not as something to go investigate.'
